#{[17592186360531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360531 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7cf5716-597c-5d80-9235-85db49003378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360531 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360549 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "574f8096-6cd3-5243-b06d-c1422dfabc1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360549 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-8)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360559 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85c2527c-a204-5bec-a809-539cc736fb33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360559 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}14-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +75 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =15,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-15\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360560 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06d1c40d-cb10-5fdd-9559-6a4e07bdb150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360560 #edb/ref 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360562 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0cd3fae-972c-5f2d-a33e-05af0737f6a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360562 #edb/ref 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394997 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218639499